<h3 class="help first">What this tool does</h3>
<p class="help">Gives back to MusicBrainz. While processing albums, MetaForge finds credits (producers, players, writers) that Discogs, AllMusic or Wikipedia list but MusicBrainz doesn't have. This tool sends them to MusicBrainz an album at a time. MetaForge never submits anything itself: you check the edit on MusicBrainz's own page and enter it.</p>

<h3>How to send an album's credits</h3>
<ol>
    <li>Choose an album from the list. <strong>Find an album or artist</strong> narrows the list; <strong>Order</strong> sorts it by most credits, artist, or newest first.</li>
    <li>MetaForge checks MusicBrainz first. Credits it already has are set aside, and each person is matched to their MusicBrainz artist.</li>
    <li>The rest are listed by role, ticked. Untick anything you don't want to send; MetaForge remembers.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Open in MusicBrainz</strong> (or press Enter). The release's relationship editor opens in a second window, and MetaForge adds the ticked credits there (up to 25 at a time) with one edit note saying where each came from.</li>
    <li>Check them on that page and click <strong>Enter edit</strong>. Then close the window. MetaForge asks whether you entered the edit, and if you did, it lists those credits as waiting.</li>
</ol>
<p class="tool_notes">The MusicBrainz window keeps you logged in, so you only log in once.</p>

<h3>What's sent</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Sources:</strong> credits from Discogs, AllMusic and Wikipedia, your own entries, and AI web search credits you checked in the verify window and rated Explicit or Inferred. AI credits that are unchecked, rated Anecdotal, or only switched to Confirmed in Personnel are never sent.</li>
    <li><strong>Roles:</strong> producer, arranger, composer, writer, lyricist, vocals, backing vocals, and named instruments. Instruments are matched to MusicBrainz's own instrument list ("Sax (Tenor)" becomes "tenor saxophone"); a role with no exact match isn't sent.</li>
    <li><strong>Artists:</strong> a credit goes out only when the person is matched for certain: by their MusicBrainz ID, or because exactly one MusicBrainz artist has that name. Otherwise MetaForge shows the likely artists and you choose (or choose "None of these").</li>
</ul>

<h3>What isn't sent, and why</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Already on MusicBrainz:</strong> found on the release, a track or a song.</li>
    <li><strong>Similar credit already there:</strong> MusicBrainz has the same person credited differently (say, "bass" where the source says "double bass"). These start unticked; tick one only if it's a different credit.</li>
    <li><strong>Only on some tracks:</strong> the source credits certain tracks only, so it isn't a credit for the whole release. Sending credits to single tracks is a later step.</li>
    <li><strong>Can't be sent:</strong> roles too general for MusicBrainz ("Featuring", "Musician") and kinds MetaForge can't send yet (engineering, mastering, artwork and so on).</li>
    <li><strong>Confirmed against a different release:</strong> if the page you confirmed a credit with names a different album, the credit starts unticked, with the link, so you can check.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Waiting for MusicBrainz</h3>
<p>Edits by newer MusicBrainz editors are voted on for up to a week before they show. MetaForge checks again each time you open the album (or choose <strong>Check MusicBrainz again</strong>), and moves a credit to "added by you" once it's there.</p>

<h3>Skipping an album</h3>
<p><strong>Skip this album</strong> hides it from the list. Tick <strong>Show skipped albums</strong> to see it again, and choose <strong>Stop skipping this album</strong> to bring it back.</p>
